ally in  the silence of the lam's screenplay but  was instead something the actor added in  at the last minute in a 2016 interview  with radio times he revealed i don't  know why but i've always known what  scares people when i was a kid i'd tell  the girls around the streets the story  about dracula he then reproduced  lecture's legendary hiss and added that  that runs screaming away  jack nicholson's performance as a writer  losing his mind in 1980s to shining is  filled with plenty of unforgettable  moments there are the quiet opening  scenes when his strange smile belies his  simmering insanity then there are  sequences when the overlook hotel begins  to seduce and increasingly boost up jack  torrance thereby alienating him from  reality itself and then there's of  course a film's final act when he tears  through anyone foolish enough to wind up  on the pointy end of his axe and the  latter features one particularly  memorable improvised moment while jack's  wife wendy cowers in the bathroom he  hacks away at the feeble door with  reckless abandon after creating a  suitable crack he peers through the  porthole and maniacally proclaims  here's johnny  that line doesn't appear in either  stephen king's novel or the film's  screenplay as it was instead ad-libbed  by nicholson maybe director stanley  kubrick's infamously exhaustive takes  drove nicholson over the deep end or  maybe he's just a really big fan of late  night host johnny carson either way it's  a terrifying moment  just imagine you're out on a shark hunt  and when you least expect it your prey  emerges from the ocean's deaths summoned  by all that delicious chum you've been  dumping into the water how would you  respond before this moment happens in  1975's jaws police chief martin brody  has seen the shark before but only in  mere glimpses but now with the beast  just feet from his face he has a real  sense of what he and his men are up  against  you're gonna need a bigger boat  as joe's screenwriter 